{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{#
	Where a confirmation link lands (XIV-64).

	**Deliberately the plainest page in the repository**, and that is a decision
	rather than an unfinished edge. [XIV-65] owns the landing page and the form;
	this is the other end of a link in an email, which only this side can answer
	because the token is a row in the control-plane database. Building anything
	marketing-shaped here would be building something that ticket has to replace.

	One centred card and no frame, the same shape the two sign-in pages use
	(XIV-79) and for the same reason: the reader has not been admitted to an
	application, so drawing one around them would be furniture. The version stays,
	inside the card, because it is the first question about any bug report.

	Five outcomes, one block each, and no `else`. A new case in
	ConfirmationOutcome should show up as a page with no message rather than as
	the wrong message — silence is a bug somebody reports, and a plausible
	sentence about the wrong thing is not.
#}
{% block title %}{{ 'signup.confirmation.title'|trans }}{% endblock %}

{% block footer %}{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
	<main class="signin-shell">
		<div class="card shadow-sm" style="width: 26rem;">
			<div class="card-body p-4 text-center">
				<h1 class="h5 mb-3">{{ 'signup.confirmation.title'|trans }}</h1>

				{% if outcome == 'Confirmed' %}
					<div class="alert alert-success py-2 small" role="alert">
						{{ 'signup.confirmation.confirmed'|trans({'%company%': signup.companyName}) }}
					</div>
					<p class="text-body-secondary small mb-0">
						{{ 'signup.confirmation.next'|trans({'%slug%': signup.slug}) }}
					</p>

				{% elseif outcome == 'AlreadyConfirmed' %}
					{#
						Not an error and not drawn as one. A second click is the
						ordinary case: people click twice, mail is forwarded, and a
						corporate link scanner fetches every URL in a message before
						its recipient has seen it.
					#}
					<div class="alert alert-info py-2 small" role="alert">
						{{ 'signup.confirmation.already'|trans({'%company%': signup.companyName}) }}
					</div>
					<p class="text-body-secondary small mb-0">
						{{ 'signup.confirmation.next'|trans({'%slug%': signup.slug}) }}
					</p>

				{% elseif outcome == 'Expired' %}
					<div class="alert alert-warning py-2 small" role="alert">
						{{ 'signup.confirmation.expired'|trans }}
					</div>
					<p class="text-body-secondary small mb-0">{{ 'signup.confirmation.sign_up_again'|trans }}</p>

				{% elseif outcome == 'SlugTaken' %}
					{#
						The narrow race the anti-squatting rule cannot design away:
						nothing is held until a confirmation, so somebody else can
						confirm the same name first. Their address is not stuck —
						signing up again replaces this row — so the instruction is
						the same one as above.
					#}
					<div class="alert alert-warning py-2 small" role="alert">
						{{ 'signup.confirmation.slug_taken'|trans({'%slug%': signup.slug}) }}
					</div>
					<p class="text-body-secondary small mb-0">{{ 'signup.confirmation.sign_up_again'|trans }}</p>

				{% elseif outcome == 'Unknown' %}
					{#
						A token that never existed, one superseded by a second
						submission, and a signup already turned into a customer are
						one answer on purpose: distinguishing them would tell
						whoever holds this URL something about a row they cannot
						otherwise see.
					#}
					<div class="alert alert-secondary py-2 small" role="alert">
						{{ 'signup.confirmation.unknown'|trans }}
					</div>
					<p class="text-body-secondary small mb-0">{{ 'signup.confirmation.sign_up_again'|trans }}</p>
				{% endif %}

				<p class="text-body-secondary small mb-0 mt-4">Xivi {{ app_version }}</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</main>
{% endblock %}
